# The four short peripheral cycles.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. See put_pc_memory_write32.espi.
#
# These are the one peripheral packet with no cycle type byte at all. Section
# 3.8, p.39: "The unique opcode indicates the type of non-posted transaction
# and the request length. The header contains the address only and the number
# of address bytes for the transaction is implied by the opcode. The short
# non-posted transaction does not have the Tag field."
#
# So there is no cycle type, no tag and no length on the wire. C1C0 in the low
# two bits of the opcode is the only statement of how many data bytes the
# packet holds, which makes it a length field that lives in the opcode table
# rather than in the packet -- and makes the reserved encoding 10b a genuinely
# undecodable packet rather than a mislabelled one. That case is in
# malformed.espi.
#
# WHERE THE DATA SITS depends on the direction of the transfer. The four forms
# are drawn across two sections, because three of them are non-posted and the
# fourth is not:
#
#   Figure 26, p.40, section 3.8 -- Non-Posted Transaction
#     PUT_IORD_SHORT     HDR(16) CRC        ->  RESPONSE Data STS CRC
#     PUT_IOWR_SHORT     HDR(16) Data CRC   ->  RESPONSE STS CRC
#     PUT_MEMRD32_SHORT  HDR(32) CRC        ->  RESPONSE Data STS CRC
#
#   Figure 29, p.42, section 3.9 -- Posted Transaction
#     PUT_MEMWR32_SHORT  HDR(32) Data CRC   ->  RESPONSE STS CRC
#
# Note what the read responses do NOT have: a completion header. Figure 24 on
# p.38 gives the long form's response a full `HDR DATA` and Figure 26 gives the
# short form's response bare data bytes. The two figures are two pages apart
# and are the obvious pair to flatten into one shape.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).

# PUT_IORD_SHORT with C1C0 = 01b -> 2 bytes. Opcode 41h, not 40h: the mask in
# Table 2 is FCh, so all four of 40h-43h are the same command with different
# request lengths. An exact-match opcode lookup reports 41h as undefined.
#
# The address is 2 bytes, MSB first -- Figure 37, p.54, right hand side.
CMD 41 00 62 C4
TAR
RSP 08 AA 55 0F 01 5A

# PUT_MEMWR32_SHORT with C1C0 = 11b -> 4 bytes. Opcode 4Fh.
#
# This one is POSTED while the other three short cycles are non-posted --
# Table 2, p.26, has it needing the target "free to take the Posted or
# Completion packet" where PUT_IOWR_SHORT, its obvious twin, says Non-Posted.
# The address is 4 bytes, MSB first, Figure 35 p.53.
CMD 4F FE D4 00 10 01 02 03 04 C3
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95
